"Naive search for 3×3 with 23 multiplications:" println
"  - Coefficient matrices: U(23×9), V(23×9), W(23×9)" println
"  - If coefficients in {-2,-1,0,1,2}: 5^(23×9×3) = 5^621" println
"  - That's ~10^434 combinations!" println
"  - Age of universe: ~10^17 seconds" println
"  - Even at 10^20 tests/sec: impossible!" println
"" println

"With constraints:" println
"" println

"1. SPARSITY CONSTRAINT:" println
"   - Most coefficients are 0 (sparse matrices)" println
"   - Strassen: only ~30% non-zero coefficients" println
"   - If we limit to ≤3 non-zeros per row:" println
"   - Reduces from 5^621 to ~C(9,3)^621 × 4^(3×621)" println
"   - Still huge but more structured" println
"" println

"2. COEFFICIENT MAGNITUDE:" println
"   - Limit to {-1, 0, 1} instead of {-2,-1,0,1,2}" println
"   - Reduces each choice from 5 to 3" println
"   - Changes 5^621 to 3^621 (~10^296)" println
"   - Still too big, but 10^138 times smaller!" println
"" println

"3. STRUCTURAL PATTERNS:" println
"   - Many algorithms use pairs: (a+b), (a-b)" println
"   - Coefficients often symmetric or antisymmetric" println
"   - Can parameterize by these patterns" println
"   - Reduces to ~10^50 meaningful configurations" println
"" println

"4. START FROM KNOWN ALGORITHMS:" println
"   - Strassen uses 7 muls for 2×2" println
"   - Laderman uses 23 muls for 3×3" println
"   - Mutate one coefficient at a time" println
"   - Local search: ~1000s of neighbors per state" println
"   - Can explore 10^6-10^9 candidates (feasible!)" println
"" println

